/// ML SURFACE PAPERCUT (2026-07-14, seed rcdzc reader, iter 40). Two lowercase common identifiers — `bin`
/// and `quote` — are GRAMMAR-FORM HEADS (`bin(…)` = the binary-matching construct; `quote(…)` = the
/// metaprogramming reifier), so a user function named `bin` or `quote` can be DEFINED and referenced as a
/// bare value, but CANNOT be CALLED with the normal `f(x)` syntax — the reader captures `bin(` / `quote(`
/// as the grammar form BEFORE resolution, and the diagnostic does not say the name is reserved.
///
///   def bin(x) = x + 1
///   bin(5)     → CDZ0201 "a bin segment must be (<kind> <slot> [modifier])"   -- NOT "bin is reserved"
///   `bin`(5)   → same error (backtick-quoting the name does NOT escape the call-position capture)
///
///   def quote(x) = x + 1     → the DEFINITION itself mis-parses: `x` is reported both unused AND unbound
///                              (the body `x + 1` is reified by `quote`, so `x` never binds).
///   quote(5)   → CDZ0101 "unbound name x"
///
/// CONTRAST — the other compound-ctor head words are NOT hijacked at call position and work fine as fn
/// names: `list(5)`, `tuple(5)`, `record(5)`, `map(5)`, `set(5)` all DEFINE + CALL + RUN correctly (they
/// are special only as compound-ctor HEADS in specific positions, not as bare `name(...)` applications).
/// So the hijack is specific to `bin` and `quote`.
///
/// WORKAROUND: don't name a function `bin` or `quote` (this port hit `bin` organically for a `Bin`-node
/// builder in `unparse.cdz` — renamed to `mkb`). Ideal: (a) a diagnostic that says "`bin`/`quote` is a
/// reserved construct name — rename the function", and/or (b) let a backtick-escaped ``bin``/``quote``
/// resolve to the user binding at call position (the escape mechanism already exists for other re-lexing
/// names like `` `->` ``).
///
/// This file shows the WORKING contrast (list/tuple/record/map/set as fn names) so it CHECKS CLEAN; the
/// hijacked `bin`/`quote` forms are in the comment above (each reproduces on its own two-line program).
def list(x: Int64) = x + 1

def tuple(x: Int64) = x + 2

def record(x: Int64) = x + 3

def map(x: Int64) = x + 4

def set(x: Int64) = x + 5

@test
def compound-head-words-work-as-fn-names() =
  // list/tuple/record/map/set are callable as ordinary functions (unlike bin/quote)
  if list(0) + tuple(0) + record(0) + map(0) + set(0) == 15 then unit else trap("1+2+3+4+5 = 15")

export { list, tuple, record, map, set }
